
A moving pointer implementation treats the search order as a true push-down stack in memory. Instead of keeping a fixed base address and a separate counter (#ORDER), the system maintains a single pointer variable (CONTEXT) that moves up and down a fixed array of cells as wordlists are added or removed.

1. Memory Layout and Pointer Mechanics

• Base Buffer (ORDER-STACK). A small fixed block of memory (e.g., 16 cells) allocated to hold active wordlist identifiers (wids).
• Stack Pointer (CONTEXT). Holds the exact memory address of the topmost active wordlist (wid1).
• Empty State. When CONTEXT equals ORDER-STACK (cell 0), the search order is empty (n = 0).
• Active Slots. As wordlists are pushed, CONTEXT advances by 1 CELLS per entry (ORDER-STACK + 1 CELLS holds widn, up to CONTEXT holding wid1).
• Instant Top Access. Because CONTEXT always points directly to wid1, replacing the top wordlist (the standard action of a VOCABULARY word) is a single memory store: wid CONTEXT !.

2. Complete Forth Implementation

Below is a complete, self-contained ANS Forth search-order implementation using the moving pointer design. It includes SET-ORDER, GET-ORDER, ALSO, PREVIOUS, ONLY, DEFINITIONS, VOCABULARY, and the interpreter lookup loop.
forth
\ --- 1. Data Structures ---
16 CONSTANT MAX-ORDER
CREATE ORDER-STACK MAX-ORDER 1+ CELLS ALLOT  \ Cell 0 is empty base; Cells 1..16 hold wids
ORDER-STACK VALUE CONTEXT                    \ Moving pointer to top active wid
0 VALUE CURRENT                              \ Compilation wordlist (wid)

\ --- 2. Core Search-Order Primitives ---
: SET-ORDER ( widn ... wid1 n -- )
   DUP -1 = IF DROP FORTH-WORDLIST 1 THEN    \ -1 resets to default search order
   DUP MAX-ORDER > IF -49 THROW THEN         \ Search-order overflow
   DUP 0<          IF -50 THROW THEN         \ Search-order underflow
   ORDER-STACK TO CONTEXT                    \ Reset pointer to empty base
   0 ?DO
      CONTEXT CELL+ TO CONTEXT               \ Advance pointer by 1 cell
      CONTEXT !                              \ Store wid1 first, ending with widn at top
   LOOP ;

\ Note: In standard stack order ( widn ... wid1 n ), wid1 is on TOP of the data stack.
\ To achieve this, SET-ORDER pops wid1 first and stores it at the highest address,
\ so we actually want widn at cell 1 and wid1 at CONTEXT!
Use code with caution.
Correction on Stack Direction: Because SET-ORDER receives ( widn ... wid1 n ) with wid1 on top of the data stack, popping wid1 first means wid1 should end up at CONTEXT (the top of the memory stack) while widn ends up at ORDER-STACK + 1 CELLS (the bottom). Writing downward from the target top address keeps both stacks aligned:
forth
: SET-ORDER ( widn ... wid1 n -- )
   DUP -1 = IF DROP FORTH-WORDLIST 1 THEN
   DUP MAX-ORDER > IF -49 THROW THEN         \ Search-order overflow
   DUP 0<          IF -50 THROW THEN         \ Search-order underflow
   CELLS ORDER-STACK + DUP TO CONTEXT        \ Set CONTEXT directly to new top slot (wid1)
   BEGIN
      DUP ORDER-STACK >                      \ While pointer is above base cell 0
   WHILE
      SWAP OVER !                            \ Pop next wid (wid1, wid2...) and store at pointer
      1 CELLS -                              \ Move pointer down toward base
   REPEAT
   DROP ;

: GET-ORDER ( -- widn ... wid1 n )
   CONTEXT ORDER-STACK - 1 CELLS /           \ Calculate active count n from pointer offset
   DUP >R                                    \ Save count n on return stack
   ORDER-STACK                               \ Start just below bottom entry (widn)
   R@ 0 ?DO
      CELL+ DUP @ SWAP                       \ Advance up to CONTEXT, fetching widn ... wid1
   LOOP
   DROP R> ;                                 \ Drop pointer, push count n on top
Use code with caution.

3. High-Level Search-Order Words

With CONTEXT pointing directly to wid1, both standard words and fast low-level optimizations work cleanly:
forth
\ Standard portable definitions:
: ONLY     ( -- ) -1 SET-ORDER ;
: ALSO     ( -- ) GET-ORDER OVER SWAP 1+ SET-ORDER ;
: PREVIOUS ( -- ) GET-ORDER NIP 1- SET-ORDER ;

\ Direct pointer equivalents (faster, avoids copying the whole stack):
: FAST-ALSO ( -- )
   CONTEXT ORDER-STACK MAX-ORDER CELLS + >= IF -49 THROW THEN
   CONTEXT @                                 \ Fetch current top wid1
   CONTEXT CELL+ TO CONTEXT                  \ Advance pointer
   CONTEXT ! ;                               \ Duplicate wid1 into new top slot

: FAST-PREVIOUS ( -- )
   CONTEXT ORDER-STACK <= IF -50 THROW THEN  \ Underflow check
   CONTEXT 1 CELLS - TO CONTEXT ;            \ Simply move pointer down 1 cell

\ Compilation target & Vocabulary defining word:
: DEFINITIONS ( -- )
   CONTEXT @ TO CURRENT ;                    \ Copy top search wid into CURRENT

: VOCABULARY ( "name" -- )
   WORDLIST CREATE ,
   DOES> @ CONTEXT ! ;                       \ Overwrite top slot directly via CONTEXT !
Use code with caution.

4. Text Interpreter Word Lookup (FIND-NAME)

When the Forth text interpreter looks up a word ( c-addr u ), it starts at CONTEXT (wid1) and walks backward down to ORDER-STACK + 1 CELLS (widn), calling SEARCH-WORDLIST on each slot until a match is found:
forth
: SEARCH-ORDER-LIST ( c-addr u -- 0 | xt 1 | xt -1 )
   CONTEXT                                   \ Start at top active slot (wid1)
   BEGIN
      DUP ORDER-STACK >                      \ Stop when pointer reaches empty base (cell 0)
   WHILE
      >R 2DUP R@ @ SEARCH-WORDLIST           \ Search wordlist at current pointer
      ?DUP IF                                \ Match found? (returns 1 or -1)
         R> DROP 2SWAP 2DROP EXIT            \ Clean up pointer and string, return xt & flag
      THEN
      R> 1 CELLS -                           \ Step pointer down to next wordlist
   REPEAT
   DROP 2DROP 0 ;                            \ Not found in any active wordlist
Use code with caution.

Summary of Benefits

• Zero Index Math at Runtime. Invoking a vocabulary (EDITOR) executes CONTEXT !, and DEFINITIONS executes CONTEXT @ TO CURRENT.
• O(1) Stack Operations. FAST-ALSO and FAST-PREVIOUS adjust a single address pointer rather than shifting array elements or copying stacks.
• Self-Contained State. The single CONTEXT pointer encodes both the active top wordlist and the current search-order depth (CONTEXT ORDER-STACK - CELL/).
